Z||000000||fropuff||05-02-2000||07:39 PM||||Okay,  I bought my Fiero a few weeks ago.  My friends have seen it, and they thought it was really cool.  Okay, so when I was at school, my friend and I were talking about it.  He is a "know-it-all", and he's talking about how dangerous it is.  First, he said how screwed I'd be if I got rear-ended, being that the engine is in the rear.  Is it just me, or wouldn't the same thing happen if you are in a head-on collision?  Then he said being that all of the weight is in the rear, the front will flip backwards when I'm going up Grand Ave. (a really steep hill here in Duluth.)  I'm positive that won't happen.  Just shows you how much people think they know.||||reg||10
Z||000001||Old Lar||05-02-2000||07:56 PM||||Give the know it all the brushoff.  The Fiero weight is evenly balanced front to back.  Thats why the car handles so well.  It rated way up there in the crash tests for safety.  The gas tank is in the middle of the car well protected, not like the 60's Rustangs or the Pintos (caution may explode on impact) Fords better idea.||||reg||4
